Here's a recommended structure:

**1. Make HTTP calls injectable**

```csharp
// In your MCP server project
public class WeatherTool
{
    private readonly HttpClient _httpClient;

    public WeatherTool(HttpClient httpClient)
    {
        _httpClient = httpClient;
    }

    public async Task<string> GetWeather(string city)
    {
        var response = await _httpClient.GetAsync($"https://api.example.com/weather?city={city}");
        var json = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
        return json;
    }
}
```

**2. Create the test project**

```bash
dotnet new xunit -n MyMcpServer.Tests
cd MyMcpServer.Tests
dotnet add reference ../MyMcpServer/MyMcpServer.csproj
dotnet add package Moq           # or use hand-rolled fakes
dotnet add package RichardSzalay.MockHttp  # great for HttpClient mocking
```

**3. Mock HTTP responses in tests**

```csharp
using RichardSzalay.MockHttp;

public class WeatherToolTests
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task GetWeather_ReturnsTemperature()
    {
        // Arrange — fake the external API
        var mockHttp = new MockHttpMessageHandler();
        mockHttp
            .When("https://api.example.com/weather?city=Seattle")
            .Respond("application/json", """{"temp": 72}""");

        var httpClient = mockHttp.ToHttpClient();
        var tool = new WeatherTool(httpClient);

        // Act
        var result = await tool.GetWeather("Seattle");

        // Assert
        Assert.Contains("72", result);
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task GetWeather_HandlesApiError()
    {
        var mockHttp = new MockHttpMessageHandler();
        mockHttp
            .When("*")
            .Respond(System.Net.HttpStatusCode.ServiceUnavailable);

        var tool = new WeatherTool(mockHttp.ToHttpClient());

        await Assert.ThrowsAsync<HttpRequestException>(
            () => tool.GetWeather("Seattle"));
    }
}
```

**Key principles:**

| Concern | Approach |
|---|---|
| **Inject `HttpClient`** | Accept it via constructor so tests can substitute a mock |
| **`MockHttpMessageHandler`** | Intercepts requests at the handler level — no real network calls |
| **Test the tool logic** | Verify parsing, error handling, and return values against canned responses |
| **Project layout** | `src/MyMcpServer/` + `tests/MyMcpServer.Tests/` |

This pattern lets you test all your tool logic (JSON parsing, error handling, response mapping) without touching the real API, and the same `HttpClient` injection works with `IHttpClientFactory` if you use that later.
